 ukraine  um is very different from  the very you know drastic  response that we'd seen at the belarus  border just just six months before  um  alexander best talk a little bit more  about the differences that you that  you're seeing in how  countries have  responded to this i mean obviously  poland has has taken the brunt of this  in in the short term uh as far as the  number of uh ukrainians that they've  taken in  but um i mean what about the ones that  are not sort of pulling their weight so  to speak  yeah we need responsibility sharing  across europe and this has been one of  the challenges faced historically by the  common european asylum system that  responsibility has been allocated  through the so-called dublin system to  the frontline countries so  in the  2015-16 crisis  greece was a front-line country and  other frontline countries like hungary  took a very restrictive view building a  razor wire fence trying to present  access to their territory now what we've  needed to do and what the european  commission has recognized is that we  need to update that system to ensure  that there's more consistent  responsibility sharing cooperation  across the eu 27 member states now this  is a moment to put that to the test to  recognize that for this to be  sustainable the responsibility can't lie  exclusively with poland and the visagrad  countries that we need to relocate both  people and share money that it will take  significant resourcing to support public  services to build jobs and livelihoods  opportunities  one of the positive things is we've got  learnings that we can take from the  aftermath of 2015-16  germany in particular which took the  overwhelming majority of syrian refugees  arriving in europe provided huge  investment to vocational training  employment integration that took time to  pay off but over time has seen more and  more syrians enter labour markets in  germany and integrate socioeconomically  if we can learn from that and have a  relocation scheme  and financial support and viable  opportunities to improve public services  employment opportunities not only for  ukrainian refugees but also local host  communities in the areas most effective  we've got the best possible chance to  have cooperation across europe but also  ensure crucially that local receiving  communities are still as responsive  enthusiastic and committed to refugee  rights in one year two years three years  as they are today  yeah um uh alexander brings up a big  point a good point there uh camille  about about the germany uh model in in  2015  2016 they took they took so many of the  refugees from from syria i think it was  over a million of them and they've  managed to integrate them into into  german society so this can be done can't  it  yeah and i think here it's really  critical to avoid the perception that  european  government are losing control and we  know this has been a major shortcoming  in 2015-16  and this could define whether you know  all this popular support that we see now  continue in the next months um and that  you know  and i think here a key question and  that's also a lesson from um from 2 15  16 and something that germany has been a  leader on is how do we make sure that  this right to work this access to  employment is is effective  um and so  again a lot of tools have been deployed  in term of you know credential  recognition that we're seeing  reactivated now and while we're all  focused on the operational response you  know the emergency um and you know the  the  make make this our instrument that has  never been used before in europe work we  need to be thinking about this medium to  long-term consideration because that's  something maybe that in 2015-16 we  didn't think about early enough  um  matthew so much i mean as i mentioned  there uh  so many so many people coming in such a  short time um are you getting a sense  now uh at all that that um uh  the the various authorities are are  getting a handle on this or could you  expect it to continue to get worse  it's very difficult to say right now  obviously that depends on events in  ukraine and the intensification there  but in terms of the government response  we think it it has been exemplary of  course  you know there there are there is going  to be an element of chaos when you're  dealing with this this scale of movement  but if you look at the case of poland  you've already got the offers of housing  we're starting to roll out very soon uh  cash assistance for refugees here in  poland and then that will go into other  countries working with some other  partners as well  and they're talking about  vocational and medical training  recognition of qualifications for  ukrainians and i think that's important  because it helps the host country as  well  um there may have been something of a  labor loss or a brain drain in poland in  particular in the last five or ten years  so going back to that example of germany  this could also be an opportunity for  poland to reskill  um and to get a lot of that talent  that's come over from ukraine  unfortunately if you look at it from the  other side  it leaves the country of ukraine in a  very difficult position because  uh so much of that talent and that  workforce and young people  will have will have come across and of  course uh the male population at the  moment as you know are engaged in combat  uh camille lacaze what um  what implications is this going to have  for for asylum policy going forward  yeah i think i yeah i think it's early  it's too early to say and again i i  think a key um a key factor is going to  be how um perceive this this response is  um i think one thing i wanted to add  also is um you know we've talked about  this the activation of this mechanism  the temperature protection directive  and what we're seeing on the ground is  the need to register people very quickly  um with this mechanism that  was never used before and that has  involved setting up specific  registration system for people you know  who are comings and um also relying on  new mechanisms that have been set up  since 2015 and 16 such as the eu asleem  agencies that's not really helping out  um the countries that need to do this  registration  so i think in a way um we're seeing a  lot of lesson learned on how to do  better that's that we've done in the  past um and hopefully this can have  implication um for you know eu islam and  migration policy in europe but i really  think we need to um understand that this  is a specific crisis compared to  other situations that europe has faced  in the past past decade  yeah what's what's your view on that uh  alexander betts this is  that this is something very specific and  the the whole issue of whether there  needs to be a broader policy changes is  something perhaps a bit more further  into the future  i mean i think europe has needed to  change its policy response to asylum  immigration for a long time and this  might be the catalyst for that change i  think there is obviously something  unique about this context of conflict on  the borders of europe but we have seen  moments in the past that parallel this  as we've already heard during the 1990s  with the conflicts in bosnia and kosovo  we saw mass influx situations into  europe and they were the moments that  triggered significant innovation in  european asylum systems they created the  temporary protection directive in 2001  for instance that is now being invoked  as we've heard for the first time since  its creation so i think it's through  moments like this that we recognize the  need to update that human displacement  is going to be one of the defining  challenges of the 21st century that we  live in an increasingly insecure world  and that we have to have national and  regional and global systems that are  adapted to that reality so i think we  shouldn't necessarily see this as as  just a one-off it is possible that we  will have other major drivers of  displacement including to europe and we  need to ensure that we prepare for now  respond to the immediate crisis but also  have systems of responsibility sharing  that can endure i think it's also  important to recognize that regions can  learn from one another so dealing with  mass influx situations is something that  regions beyond europe have a lot of  experience with  um in africa for instance there have  been large-scale responses to movements  of south sudanese refugees somali  refugees that involved  supporting public services supporting  livelihoods and employment creation for  both refugees and host communities and  very recently in latin america the  venezuela situation is one we can learn  from colombia has taken in similar  numbers of refugees to those in poland  and it very quickly provided them access  to public services a temporary status um  the right to work and so i think there's  a lot of learning that can take place  and this is a moment where we can update  uh our european refugee response  hopefully not only for this situation  but others in future  matthew saltmarsh what are some of the  uh logistical challenges perhaps that  your organization has has been  encountering uh communication  uh down there if that's been difficult  at all what what what are some of those  challenges  well obviously inside ukraine there are  huge logistical challenges we've just  started in recent days to move convoys  of aid  across the border into the western part  of the country and then trying to get  that out into the more conflict affected  areas  the teams that we have there have been  trying to get out when they can it's not  always of course been safe to do so and  they're trying to start with uh  monitoring so to locate where internally  displaced people are set up shelter  structures and then bring the basic  support through to them  on this side of the border i think the  challenge in the first week was the  sheer scale of arrivals  people obviously were passing through  structures weren't  put up in advance of that  and  a number of people were just moving on  without being traced or without  perhaps the authorities knowing where  they're going so  trying to bring in a little bit of  order into that you mentioned children  and child protections are trying to  bring in more specialists and get in  place those important  steps that are needed in terms of  identity identification and tracing and  so on um  yeah so so that's the focus really for  now  we're gonna have to leave it there  thanks very much to all three of you  matthew saltmash camille lacaze and  alexander best thanks very much for  being on inside story thank you as  always for watching remember you can see  this program again anytime just go to  our website aljazeera.com and for more  discussion go to our facebook page  facebook.com forward slash aj inside  story you can also join the conversation  on twitter our handle there is at aj  inside story for me hasn't seeker and  the whole team here bye for now  [Music]
 

